  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLake effect showers will continue through much of the weekend\nthanks to persistent upper level troughing. Precip type will\nstart off as a cold rain or a rain/snow/graupel mix early this\nmorning, but extensive cloud cover and air coming off of\nrelatively warm Lake Erie will result in marginal temperatures\nbetween roughly 33 and 37 degrees at inland locations downwind from\nLake Erie (and even warmer temperatures along the lakeshore). \nShowers will also be rather cellular in nature so do not \nanticipate any appreciable snow accumulation outside of some \nslush on grassy surfaces. Any snow will transition to rain as \ntemperatures warm after sunrise today, but graupel can't be\nruled out in any heavier showers that develop later today.\n\n\nShowers may pick up in intensity as flow becomes a bit more \nwesterly and a better moisture connection to Lake Huron is \nestablished By this point, there will likely be a primary band \nof rain across far NE OH and possibly into NW PA with scattered\nlake effect showers to the east and west. In general, rain \namounts will remain below half an inch, but locally higher \namounts are possible where the Lake Huron band persists. \nTemperatures will likely remain above freezing in most locations\ntonight so expecting a wet rain, but can't rule out a few \nsnowflakes mixing with rain across typical cold spots in \ninterior NW PA overnight.\n\nThe upper trough will take some time to push east on Sunday so\nshowers will likely linger across the snowbelt region, but\nanticipate a downward trend in coverage and intensity throughout\nthe day Sunday as drier air filters in from the west. Any\nlingering showers should diminish as high pressure takes control\nlate Sunday night or early Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will build east over the area early next week and\npersist for several days, resulting in dry weather Monday \nthrough at least Friday. Temperatures will gradually increase\nwith slightly below normal highs in the upper 40s to around 50\ndegrees giving way to above normal highs in the upper 50s and\nlower 60s by Friday as the high shifts east.",
